    {
      "dom_id": "question-115131",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1?$",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=9$ and ${b}^{2}=36.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=9,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{9}=3.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=36,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{36}=6.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $b=6,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=3.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 6=12,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 3=6.$",
